  Google ML Kit text recognition 16.0.1: proprietary, Google ML Kit terms, https://developers.google.com/ml-kit/terms

Copyleft code inside a built library (unverified, see "Open licensing questions" below)
  espeak-ng: GPL-3.0-or-later, https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng
  The native library in the sherpa-onnx AAR contains espeak-ng phonemizer code (strings such as "phondata" and "mbrola").

Voices downloaded on first use, not shipped in the APK (from https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx/releases/tag/tts-models)
  Kokoro (int8, English v0.19): Apache-2.0, per the model card on Hugging Face (hexgrad/Kokoro-82M, checked 2026-11-14).
  Piper en_US libritts_r medium: voices trained on LibriTTS-R, CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Credit: LibriTTS-R, Y. Koizumi et al.; Piper voice by Rhasspy, https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices (not verified here).
  espeak-ng-data inside both model archives: GPL-3.0-or-later.

Web services the app calls at run time
  Project Gutenberg catalogue, reduced daily from its bulk feed and published to this repository's release `catalogue`: see docs/gutenberg.md. Open Library book lookup by ISBN (one request per user action; its API page asks regular callers to send a User-Agent with a contact, which this call does with the project URL, and says the APIs are not a backend for third-party services).

Open licensing questions (not legal advice; unresolved)
  1. A built APK would combine MPL-2.0 code, GPL-3.0 espeak-ng code and proprietary ML Kit libraries. GPL-3.0 does not
     allow a proprietary library in the same combined work, so do not publish an APK until this is resolved.
  2. The source repository itself carries none of these binaries.
